{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788236681862,"sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_e4b6dde4120242aa92603438","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"./venv/bin/python << 'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\n\n# Check seasons per file\ndef seasons(path, col):\n    s = collections.Counter()\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f): s[row[col]] += 1\n    return dict(sorted(s.items()))\n\nprint('Cup seasons:', seasons('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', 'season'))\nprint('Lib seasons:', seasons('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', 'season'))\nprint('BRFB Copa do Brasil years:')\nyrs = collections.Counter()\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        if row['tournament'] == 'Copa do Brasil': yrs[row['date'][:4]] += 1\nprint(dict(sorted(yrs.items())))\nEOF"},"output":"Cup seasons: {'2012': 112, '2013': 158, '2014': 159, '2015': 158, '2016': 162, '2017': 120, '2018': 120, '2019': 120, '2020': 120, '2021': 108}\nLib seasons: {'2013': 126, '2014': 126, '2015': 126, '2016': 126, '2017': 126, '2018': 126, '2019': 125, '2020': 125, '2021': 124, '2022': 124, 'NA': 1}\nBRFB Copa do Brasil years:\n{'2014': 43, '2015': 139, '2016': 147, '2017': 105, '2018': 111, '2019': 117, '2020': 117, '2021': 111, '2022': 117, '2023': 116}\n","metadata":{"output":"Cup seasons: {'2012': 112, '2013': 158, '2014': 159, '2015': 158, '2016': 162, '2017': 120, '2018': 120, '2019': 120, '2020': 120, '2021': 108}\nLib seasons: {'2013': 126, '2014': 126, '2015': 126, '2016': 126, '2017': 126, '2018': 126, '2019': 125, '2020': 125, '2021': 124, '2022': 124, 'NA': 1}\nBRFB Copa do Brasil years:\n{'2014': 43, '2015': 139, '2016': 147, '2017': 105, '2018': 111, '2019': 117, '2020': 117, '2021': 111, '2022': 117, '2023': 116}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python << 'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\n\n# Check seasons per file\ndef seasons(path, col):\n    s = collections.Counter()\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f): s[row[col]] += 1\n    return dict(sorted(s.items()))\n\nprint('Cup seasons:', seasons('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', 'season'))\nprint('Lib seasons:', seasons('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', 'season'))\nprint('BRFB Copa do Brasil years:')\nyrs = collections.Counter()\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        if row['tournament'] == 'Copa do Brasil': yrs[row['date'][:4]] += 1\nprint(dict(sorted(yrs.items())))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788236681858,"end":1788236681861}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's a row with NA values in Libertadores (2020 final second leg). Need to handle missing data gracefully.\n\nLet me also check the Brasileirão 2012-2022 file — is 2022 season complete? And the Copa do Brasil finals (round 8?). Also check the hist file \"Vencedor\" values. Let me plan the implementation now.\n\n## Design\n\nI'll build a Python MCP server using the official `mcp` package (FastMCP / stdio server), with a clean layered architecture:\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n├── __init__.py\n├── models.py          # dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamStats\n├── normalizer.py      # team name normalization (accent stripping, state suffix removal, aliasing)\n├── loaders.py         # CSV loaders for all 6 files → unified Match/Player objects\n├── service.py         # query API: match queries, team stats, h2h, standings, players, analytics\n└── server.py          # MCP tool definitions (FastMCP)\nserver.py              # entrypoint (or main.py)\ntests/\n├── test_normalizer.py\n├── test_loaders.py\n├── test_service_matches.py\n├── test_service_teams.py\n├── test_service_players.py\n├── test_service_competitions.py\n├── test_service_stats.py\n└── test_server.py     # MCP tool smoke tests via in-memory session\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update at end)\n```\n\nKey components:\n\n### 1. Team name normalization\n- Strip accents, lowercase, remove state suffixes (\"-SP\", \" - RJ\"), remove common suffixes (\"FC\", \"EC\", \"Sport Club\", \"Futebol Clube\"...), map aliases (\"Atletico-MG\" == \"Atletico Mineiro\" == \"Atlético-MG\" == \"America-MG\" etc.). Also handle \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atletico-PR\" (they're the same club - Athletico-PR renamed from Atletico-PR in 2019... Actually in the data, `novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv` has both 'Athletico-PR' and 'Atlético-PR'?). Hmm, careful: Athletico-PR (Paranaense) renamed in 2019. Atlético-PR in Brasileirao_Matches is the same club. Let me verify in the data: Brasileirao_Matches has 'Atletico-PR' (no accent). Hist has 'Athletico-PR' AND 'Atlético-GO', 'Atlético-MG'. So yes, Athletico-PR and Atletico-PR are the same club (Club Athletico Paranaense). But \"Atletico-MG\" (Atlético Mineiro) is different from \"Atletico-PR\". Need careful aliasing:\n  - \"atletico-mg\" → Atlético Mineiro (also \"Atletico Mineiro\" in BRFB)\n  - \"atletico-pr\" / \"athletico-pr\" / \"athletico paranaense\" / \"atletico goianiense\"? No — Atlético-GO is Atlético Goianiense (\"Atletico Goianiense\" in BRFB). \n  - \"America-MG\" vs \"América - MG\" vs \"America MG\".\n  - \"Avai\" vs \"Avaí-SC\" — strip accent + state suffix → \"avai\".\n  - \"Criciuma-SC\" → \"criciuma\"; hist might have \"Criciúma\".\n  - \"Goias\" vs \"Goiás\".\n  - FIFA club names: \"Flamengo\", \"Palmeiras\" — Brazilian clubs in FIFA 19: Flamengo, Palmeiras are in there. \n\nNormalization algorithm:\n1. Decode UTF-8, strip whitespace.\n2. Remove state suffix: regex `-([A-Z]{2})$` or ` - ([A-Z]{2})$`.\n3. Remove parenthetical country/club notes: \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → strip parenthesized parts.\n4. Unaccent (NFKD → ASCII).\n5. Lowercase.\n6. Apply alias map for known equivalences (atletico-pr/athletico-pr → athletico paranaense; \"corinthians\" ↔ \"sport club corinthians paulista\"; \"gremio\" ↔ \"porto alegre\"? No, keep simple).\n7. Also remove trailing club-words? E.g., \"Fortaleza Esporte Clube\" → \"fortaleza\". I'll do token-based filtering: remove tokens in {fc, ec, sc, ac, ca, clube, club, esporte, futebol, sport, sporte, athletic?, ...} — careful with \"Sport-PE\" (Sport Recife is a club named \"Sport\"!). Hmm. \"Sport-PE\" → strip suffix → \"sport\". If I remove the token \"sport\", \"Sport-PE\" becomes empty. So: only strip these filler tokens if a non-filler token remains.\n\nFor matching user queries → canonical team: build a registry of all known team names across files, normalized; then look up by normalized form. Provide `resolve_team(query)` returning canonical display name + matched variants.\n\n### 2. Date parsing\nHandle: \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"2023-09-24\", \"29/03/2003\". Use a `parse_date` helper trying multiple formats. Missing/NA → None.\n\n### 3. Unified Match model\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    date: date | None\n    home: str          # normalized display name\n    away: str\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    competition: str   # 'Brasileirão Série A', 'Copa do Brasil', 'Copa Libertadores', 'Série B', 'Série C', 'Brasileirão (2003–2019)'\n    source: str        # file name\n    season: int | None\n    round: str | None\n    venue: str | None  # Arena (hist only)\n    stage: str | None  # Libertadores\n    # optional extended stats\n    home_corners/away_corners/home_shots/away_shots/home_attacks/away_attacks: int|None\n    half_time_home/half_time_away: int|None\n```\n\nWait — there's overlap: Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022) and novo_campeonato (2003-2019) both have Brasileirão Série A; BR-Football-Dataset has Serie A 2014-2023. Should I dedupe? For standings computations, duplicates would double-count. Approach: tag each match with its source; competition queries default to a preferred source (Brasileirao_Matches for 2012-2022, novo for 2003-2011, BRFB for 2023). I'll implement dedup logic: a canonical \"Brasileirão\" competition view that prefers Brasileirao_Matches, falls back to novo for <2012, and BRFB Serie A for 2023. Dedup by (date, home, away, score) with normalized names.\n\nActually, simpler and more robust: keep all matches with a `competition` field distinguishing source datasets:\n- `brasileirao` (from Brasileirao_Matches.csv)\n- `brasileirao_historic` (novo_campeonato, 2003-2019)\n- `serie_a_brfootball` (BRFB Serie A)\n- `copa_do_brasil` (Brazilian_Cup_Matches)\n- `copa_do_brasil_brfootball` (BRFB Copa do Brasil)\n- `serie_b`, `serie_c` (BRFB)\n- `libertadores` (Libertadores_Matches)\n\nThen provide `competition` filter values for user-facing: \"Brasileirão\", \"Copa do Brasil\", \"Libertadores\", \"Série B\", \"Série C\". And a deduped Brasileirão dataset for standings: use brasileirao source (2012+), historic for 2003-2011, BRFB for 2023.\n\nHmm, but that's getting complex. Let me think about what queries need:\n- \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → standings from Brasileirao_Matches (38 rounds × 20 teams, complete) → Flamengo. If I mixed in novo (also 2019, complete) and BRFB Serie A 2019, I'd double count. So dedupe needed for standings.\n\nPlan: `service.standings(competition='brasileirao', season=2019)` uses a canonical, deduplicated match set. Dedup key: (season, date, home_norm, away_norm, home_goals, away_goals). When duplicates across sources, prefer source order: Brasileirao_Matches > novo > BRFB. Hmm wait, for 2019 both Brasileirao_Matches and novo have full seasons — dates should mostly match (one has time, one is DD/MM/YYYY). Scores equal. So dedup by (date, home, away, score) works. But if one source has a match on a different recorded date... risky. Alternative for standings: pick ONE source per season. For brasileirao: if season >= 2012 → Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022); elif season >= 2003 → novo; season == 2023 → BRFB Serie A. That's deterministic and correct. For copa_do_brasil: Brazilian_Cup_Matches (2012-2023?). Check seasons. For libertadores: Libertadores_Matches.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: novo file 2012-2019 duplicates Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2019. For \"find matches between X and Y\" queries, dedup would be nice too, else user sees duplicates. I'll implement dedup for match listings as well: dedupe by (date, home, away) keeping first occurrence by source preference — but careful, Brasileirao_Matches and novo may differ on scores for same match? Unlikely (both official results). And Copa do Brasil appears in both Brazilian_Cup_Matches and BRFB (2023). Dedup by (date, home, away).\n\nHmm wait — two-legged cup ties can have the same pairing twice in a season on different dates, fine. Same pairing same date twice can't happen. OK.\n\nBut one more: home/away might be swapped between sources for the same match? Unlikely for same date. And dates: novo has DD/MM/YYYY (no time), Brasileirao_Matches has datetime with time. date-only comparison works. BRFB date \"2023-09-24\" fine.\n\nEdge: Brazilian_Cup_Matches round values 1-8: round 8 = final? With 14 matches at round 8? That seems odd — 14 matches at round 8... Actually Copa do Brasil has 8 rounds in some years? Let me not overthink; \"final\" = highest round for a season. I'll compute finals as matches in the max round of each season. Hmm, but 14 matches in round 8 across seasons — if round 8 is the final (2 legs), 2012-2023 = ~12 seasons × 2 legs = 24... not exactly. Let me check quickly which seasons have round 8 and how many matches.\n\nActually, let me check that now. Also Libertadores has explicit stage='final'.\n\n### 4. MCP tools (the LLM does NL → tool calls)\nTools with clear docstrings and typed params:\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=20)` \n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)`\n3. `team_record(team, season=None, competition=None, home_away='all')` → W/D/L, GF/GA\n4. `standings(competition, season)` → table\n5. `champion(competition, season)` → winner (top of table; for cups → finalist winner via final matches)\n6. `relegated(competition, season)` → bottom 4\n7. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`\n8. `average_goals(competition=None, season=None, team=None)` + home/away win rates\n9. `best_records(venue='home'|'away', competition=None, season=None)` \n10. `derbies(season=None)` → known derby pairs (Fla-Flu, Grenal, Majestoso, Clássico dos Milhões, etc.) — define a static list of rivalries with normalized names, plus team state-based (same state teams? too broad). I'll define ~15 famous derbies.\n11. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, order_by='overall', limit=20)`\n12. `player_profile(name)` → full attributes\n13. `team_players(club)` → squad list from FIFA data (cross-file: team + player data)\n14. `brazilian_players_abroad(top=10)` / `top_brazilian_players`\n15. `club_summary(club)` → cross-file: FIFA squad + match stats\n16. `list_competitions()` / `list_seasons(competition)`\n17. `team_info(team)` → aliases, competitions played, seasons, overall record\n\nAlso a `competitions_played(team)`.\n\nThat covers the 20+ sample questions.\n\nResponse format: The spec's example answer formats are human-readable text. MCP tools should return structured but formatted text. I'll return markdown-ish formatted strings (like the examples) — LLM-friendly. Maybe return both: formatted text. Keep it simple: return formatted strings.\n\n### 5. Loading & performance\nTotal rows ~42k — tiny. Load at server startup into memory. Simple lookups are list scans — fine (<50ms). For performance requirement (<2s), trivially met. I'll add a few indexes (team → matches dict) for speed anyway.\n\n### 6. Testing — BDD GWT with pytest\nThe spec asks for BDD GWT structured tests. I'll write pytest tests using Given/When/Then structure with comments/docstrings, e.g.:\n\n```python\nclass TestMatchQueries:\n    \"\"\"Feature: Match Queries\n\n    Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n      Given the match data is loaded\n      When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n      Then I should receive a list of matches\n      And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n    \"\"\"\n    def test_find_matches_between_two_teams(self, service):\n        # Given\n        ...\n```\n\nI'll use a session-scoped fixture loading the real data. Tests assert real-data facts (Flamengo won 2019 Brasileirão with 90 pts — that's in the example and the data should confirm; but I must verify against actual data, not just spec claims). Let me verify a few facts with quick data checks before writing assertions.\n\nAlso test the MCP server tools via `mcp` client in-memory. With mcp 2.1.1, FastMCP: `mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP`. In-memory testing: use `Client(server)` from `mcp.client.session` with `mcp.client.stdio` not needed — there's `mcp.shared.memory` create_connected_server_and_client_session. In mcp 2.x, `from mcp.shared.memory import create_connected_server_and_client_session`. Let me check what's available in this version.\n\nLet me now verify key data facts for test assertions:\n1. 2019 Brasileirão standings: Flamengo champion? points?\n2. Flamengo vs Fluminense all-time in Brasileirao_Matches h2h counts.\n3. Neymar overall 92 (already saw).\n4. Biggest win in data.\n5. Copa do Brasil 2023 winner (BRFB has 2023 Copa do Brasil; Brazilian_Cup_Matches seasons?).\n6. Libertadores finals winners: 2019 Flamengo beat River Plate (seen above), 2020 Palmeiras 1-0 Santos (seen, plus NA second leg).\n\nAlso need to decide about the NA Libertadores row: skip rows with unparseable scores (Athletico vs Flamengo 2020 final leg 2 with NA). Actually the 2020 (played Jan 2021) final: Palmeiras 1-0 Santos (single match? The row \"2020 NA Flamengo - - Athletico\" is the 2020 semifinal? no wait — 2020 final was Palmeiras vs Santos single match in Jan 2021 (Maracanã). And Flamengo vs Athletico... hmm that NA row: 2020 Copa Libertadores was concluded Jan 2021: semis Palmeiras-Argentina? No: Semis: Palmeiras vs Argentinos? Actually 2020 semis: Palmeiras beat Argentinos Juniors? Let me not care — the row has NA date and NA goals; it's Flamengo vs Athletico — that was... 2020 Libertadores semifinal? No, Flamengo was eliminated by Racing? Whatever — I'll handle NA rows by skipping matches with missing scores but maybe keep them with None? Simpler: skip rows with unparseable dates AND unparseable scores for match search; but \"When did Flamengo last play Athletico\" — that NA row is a real semifinal (played 2021-01-05ish, Flamengo won on penalties? no...). Hmm, actually the 2020 Libertadores semifinals: Palmeiras vs Argentinos Juniors? No...\n\nI'll just skip rows where both date and goals are NA — cleaner. Actually keep matches with None goals but valid date (some datasets may have scheduled matches), and skip rows with NA date entirely? The row is `NA, Flamengo, -, Athletico` — date NA, goals NA. Skip it. Fine.\n\nNow check FIFA 19: \"Gabriel Barbosa\" — in FIFA 19 he's at... Inter Milan? Gabigol was at Inter in FIFA 19. Search will find him by name anyway.\n\nLet me verify facts now with quick scripts, then write the code.\n\nAlso check: Brazilian_Cup_Matches seasons range. And whether Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2022 + novo 2003-2019 + BRFB 2014-2023 — for a unified \"Brasileirão\" competition: 2003-2011 (novo), 2012-2022 (Brasileirao_Matches), 2023 (BRFB Serie A).\n\nWait, but also: does BRFB Serie A 2014-2022 duplicate? Yes but we prefer primary sources.\n\nFor standings of Série B/Série C use BRFB directly. For Copa do Brasil: Brazilian_Cup_Matches 2012-2023(?), BRFB Copa do Brasil 2023(?). Check seasons in each.\n\nLet me also confirm hist file columns for state: \"Mandante_UF\".\n\nOne more design point — the \"knowledge graph interface\" phrasing: the spec title says \"knowledge graph interface\" but the requirements only need queries. I'll model entities (Team, Match, Player, Competition) in the service layer and can add a `graph_overview`/entity-relationship flavor: e.g., `team_info` shows related entities. I won't use Neo4j (prompts.txt suggests variations exist but TASK.md is authoritative — it doesn't mandate a graph DB; in-memory knowledge model satisfies it). I'll note the knowledge-graph-style entity model in README.\n\nLet me verify data facts now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b365220001fgOAO44rwpOMrj","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","messageID":"msg_05b349f4e001yyb6aze6MCy099"}}
