\n# count Brazilian players\nbr = [r for r in rows if r['Nationality'] == 'Brazil']\nprint('Brazilian players:', len(br))\nprint('Top 5 BR:', [(r['Name'], r['Overall'], r['Club']) for r in sorted(br, key=lambda x: -int(x['Overall']))[:5]])\n# positions available\nprint('Positions:', sorted({r['Position'] for r in rows if r['Position']})[:30])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788236919654,"end":1788236919671}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good. FIFA 19 has Brazilian clubs: Atlético Mineiro, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, Botafogo, Bahia, Atlético Paranaense, Sport Club do Recife, Chapecoense, Ceará Sporting Club (and probably Flamengo, Palmeiras, São Paulo, Grêmio, Corinthians, Vasco — wait, they didn't show because my filter checked lowercase substring... 'Flamengo' contains 'flamengo'... it should have matched! Unless FIFA 19 doesn't have Flamengo/Palmeiras/São Paulo/Grêmio/Corinthians/Vasco. FIFA 19 Brazilian clubs were limited ( Brasileirão wasn't fully licensed in FIFA 19 — only some clubs with generic? Actually FIFA 19 did NOT include Brazilian league clubs except in Copa Libertadores mode? Hmm, no. The 2018 dataset is FIFA 19: Brazilian clubs in FIFA 19: Palmeiras, Flamengo, São Paulo, Santos, Grêmio, Sport, Vitória, Bahia, Atlético-PR... hmm but the scan shows only clubs listed above.\n\nWait — my scan filtered `any(b.lower() in unaccent(c).lower())` — 'Flamengo' should match club 'Flamengo'. It's not in output, so FIFA 19 data here doesn't have Flamengo/Palmeiras/SPFC/Grêmio/Corinthians/Vasco/Cruzeiro... but Cruzeiro IS there. So FIFA 19 (this dataset) includes: Atlético Mineiro, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, Botafogo, Bahia, Atlético Paranaense, Sport Club do Recife, Chapecoense, Ceará Sporting Club. Interesting — Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Grêmio, Vasco were NOT in FIFA 19 (they had Konami PES exclusivity!). Yes — that's right: in FIFA 19, Flamengo, Corinthians, Palmeiras, São Paulo, Grêmio, Vasco, Santos? no Santos IS here. PES 2019 had exclusives: Flamengo, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco, Palmeiras, Grêmio, Bahia? no Bahia is here... Whatever — the data says what it says.\n\nSo for \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" — FIFA data has no Flamengo squad. team_players(\"Flamengo\") would return empty. That's a data reality; my tools should handle gracefully and mention it. But \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" is in the spec's sample questions... The spec says \"Filter FIFA data by Club containing 'Flamengo'\" — result would be empty in this dataset. Tests should reflect reality: use clubs that exist (e.g., \"Santos\", \"Atlético Mineiro\"). I'll make the tool return a clear \"no players found at X in FIFA dataset\" message, possibly suggesting nearby clubs. And test with real clubs like Santos (Neymar is at PSG in FIFA 19; Santos has its own players).\n\nAlso, cross-file query: \"Show me all forwards from São Paulo FC\" → empty. Use \"Sport Club do Recife\" or \"Bahia\" in tests instead.\n\nNote FIFA club \"Atlético Paranaense\" → alias to (\"atletico\",\"pr\") → id atletico-pr. \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → base \"ceara sporting club\"... my token filter should strip \"sporting club\"? Generic algorithm: after extracting state, remove trailing filler words: {fc, ec, sc, ac, ca, clube, club, esporte, futebol, sporting?, sport?} — DANGER: \"Sport Club do Recife\" would become \"do recife\" — bad. Alias map better: \"ceara sporting club\" → ceara; \"sport club do recife\" → sport-pe; \"atletico mineiro\" → atletico-mg; \"atletico paranaense\" → atletico-pr; \"athletic club\"? BRFB \"Athletic Club MG\" → alias \"athletic club\" → athletic-mg (a real Série C club, distinct from atlético-mg!).\n\nI'll build the alias map by scanning registry. Implementation plan for normalizer:\n\n```python\nFILLER_TOKENS = {\"fc\",\"ec\",\"sc\",\"ac\",\"ca\",\"clube\",\"club\",\"esporte\",\"esportes\",\"futebol\",\"futebool\",\"atlético\"}  # careful\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me define explicit alias resolution:\n\n```\nALIASES = {\n  # full name (normalized, no state) -> canonical id\n  \"atletico mineiro\": \"atletico-mg\",\n  \"atletico paranaense\": \"atletico-pr\",\n  \"athletico paranaense\": \"atletico-pr\",\n  \"atletico goianiense\": \"atletico-go\",\n  \"america mineiro\": \"america-mg\",\n  \"vasco da gama\": \"vasco-rj\",\n  \"sport club do recife\": \"sport-pe\",\n  \"sport recife\": \"sport-pe\",\n  \"ceara sporting club\": \"ceara\",\n  \"botafogo de futebol e regatas\": \"botafogo-rj\",\n  \"gremio foot ball porto alegrense\": \"gremio\",\n  \"sao paulo fc\": \"sao paulo\",\n  ...\n}\nDEFAULT_STATE = {  # for ambiguous bare bases\n  \"flamengo\": \"rj\", \"santos\": \"sp\", \"botafogo\": \"rj\", \"internacional\": \"rs\",\n  \"america\": \"mg\", \"atletico\": \"mg\", \"athletico\": \"pr\", \"guarani\": \"sp\",\n  \"bragantino\": \"sp\", \"operario\": \"ms\", \"santa cruz\": \"pe\", \"nautico\": \"pe\",\n  \"juventude\": \"rs\", \"vitoria\": \"ba\", \"vasco\": \"rj\", ...\n}\n```\n\nWait but if I make bare \"flamengo\" → \"flamengo-rj\" always, then Copa do Brasil's \"Flamengo-PI\" stays \"flamengo-pi\" (has state, distinct). \n\nBut hold on — hist file 'Guarani' bare (65 matches) = Guarani-SP? And \"Guarani-CE\" in Copa do Brasil. Also BRFB? Let me not over-engineer: bare ambiguous → DEFAULT_STATE mapping. Guarani bare (hist) → guarani-sp correct (Guarani SP played Série A/B/C 2003-2019; Guarani-CE only appears in Copa do Brasil with suffix).\n\nAlso \"vasco\" bare → vasco-rj; hist has \"Vasco\" bare and \"Vasco da gama rj\" in BRFB → vasco-rj. And there could be \"Vasco-AC\"? (Rio Branco? no). Fine.\n\nAlso unambiguous bases keep id = base (\"corinthians\", \"palmeiras\", \"cruzeiro\", \"gremio\", \"saopaulo\"...). But wait — should famous clubs have ids like \"flamengo-rj\" while others are \"corinthians\"? Slightly inconsistent but harmless: ids are internal. Display names curated.\n\nHmm, actually simpler and MORE consistent: id = base-state ALWAYS when state known (flamengo-rj, corinthians-sp...), and bare → default state if ambiguous else base-only id. Then \"Corinthians-SP\" → corinthians-sp but bare \"Corinthians\" → \"corinthians\" — MISMATCH. Need canonicalization: if id base-only exists and also base-state variants exist, they must unify. \n\nCleanest: id = base if the base is UNambiguous across the entire registry; else id = base-state (with default state applied for bare ambiguous). So:\n- \"Corinthians-SP\" → strip state → base \"corinthians\" unambiguous → id \"corinthians\". ✓\n- \"Flamengo-RJ\" → base \"flamengo\" ambiguous → id \"flamengo-rj\"; bare \"Flamengo\" → default rj → \"flamengo-rj\". \"Flamengo-PI\" → \"flamengo-pi\". ✓\n- \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico-mg\"; \"Atlético Mineiro\" (alias) → atletico-mg ✓; \"Atlético-PR\" → atletico-pr ✓; \"Athletico\" bare → default pr → atletico-pr ✓ (spelling \"athletico\" maps to base \"atletico\"?? No! \"athletico\" ≠ \"atletico\" as strings. Alias: \"athletico\" → id atletico-pr. Also BRFB \"Athletico Paranaense\" → alias → atletico-pr. And \"Athletic Club MG\" → \"athletic\" — alias \"athletic club\"→athletic-mg, or base \"athletic club\" + state MG → id \"athletic-mg\". OK.)\n\nBut wait: unambiguous determination is registry-wide dynamic. Copa do Brasil tiny clubs create the ambiguity (flamengo-pi etc.). Good — dynamic determination from data is exactly right: after loading all names, compute ambiguous bases.\n\nEdge: \"santos\" ambiguous (santos-sp, santos-ap) → bare \"Santos\" → santos-sp (default). ✓ Libertadores \"Santos\" (bare) → santos-sp ✓. But foreign \"Santos Laguna\" (FIFA) — different base, fine. Hmm, FIFA \"Santos\" club → santos-sp ✓ matches team \"Santos\" in matches — good for cross-file!\n\nAlso foreign clubs with same base? \"Nacional (URU)\" vs \"Nacional (PAR)\" vs \"Nacional\"?? Libertadores has \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Nacional (PAR)\", and maybe \"Nacional\" bare (Ec