BRFB-only years 2022-2023 kept ✓.\n   Série A: Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2022 + novo 2003-2019 + BRFB 2014-2023: key (comp, season, date, home, away) → 2012-2019 duplicates between Brasileirao_Matches and novo: same date? Brasileirao_Matches datetime \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" → date 2012-05-19; novo \"19/05/2012\" → same date ✓ dedup works if home/away ids equal ✓ (e.g. \"Palmeiras-SP\" ↔ \"Palmeiras\" both → \"palmeiras\" ✓).\n   BUT: postponed/rescheduled matches may have different recorded dates between sources → duplicates survive. How many? Let me measure after implementation; if a few remain, standings might double-count a couple of matches per season. Hmm — risky for standings correctness (2019 Flamengo 90pts test!). Extra safety for standings: source-priority selection per season: for Série A standings use matches from ONE preferred source per season. I'll implement `league_matches(competition, season)` that picks the best source (max match count from a single source, priority order) — deterministic and correct. Dedup only affects general match listings.\n6. Build indexes; parse FIFA players.\n\nAlso compute display names on Match: registry.display(canonical_id) at build time.\n\nFIFA club matching: build fifa_clubs: normalized club string (strip accents lower) → club raw name; and map to team registry ids for cross-file queries: e.g. fifa \"Atlético Mineiro\" → parse → atletico-mg; fifa \"Santos\" → parse → (\"santos\", None) → after finalize: bare santos → ambiguous default sp → \"santos-sp\" ✓! But wait — FIFA clubs are NOT registered in the registry (registering them would pollute bases: \"FC Barcelona\" base barcelona bare + foreign barcelona-equ... base differs (\"barcelona\" vs foreign marker) — by_base key \"barcelona\" states {None, \"foreign:equ\"} → brazilian states of base barcelona = {} → unambiguous → canonical \"barcelona\" — that MERGES FIFA FC Barcelona with... no wait, foreign ids never merge: in finalize loop, foreign pids map to themselves ✓ and bare → base \"barcelona\" ✓ — they stay DISTINCT even if registered! Interesting. But registering FIFA clubs WOULD change ambiguity sets: base \"vasco\" states {rj} + FIFA \"Vasco da Gama\" → alias → (\"vasco\", None) → bare → still unambiguous ✓. base \"sport\": FIFA \"Sport Club do Recife\" bare + \"sport-pe\" ✓ unambiguous. base \"internacional\": FIFA \"Internacional\" bare → but states {rs, sc} already ambiguous ✓ fine.\n   \n   Hmm — registering FIFA clubs is actually CORRECT for cross-file linking (\"Which players play for Santos?\" → team_players(team_id santos-sp) → fifa club \"Santos\"). I'll register FIFA club names in the registry too (they're clubs in the ecosystem). But do NOT create matches from them. And team_info counts competitions from matches only.\n   \n   BUT danger: FIFA has \"Athletic Club de Bilbao\" → parse: no state (Bilbao?) → base \"athletic club de bilbao\" — distinct ✓. \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → trailing filler strip: \"club\"→\"sporting\"→ base \"ceara\" ✓ merges with match-data Ceará ✓. \"Ceará Sporting Club\" — wait parse: \"Sporting Kansas City\"? base \"sporting kansas city\" ✓ distinct. \"Sporting CP\": base \"sporting cp\"?? trailing \"cp\" not filler ✓ distinct base ✓.\n   FIFA \"América (MX)\"?? if exists: paren foreign MX → base \"america\" + foreign \"mx\" → id \"america-mx\" — distinct from america-mg ✓ NICE.\n   FIFA \"Botafogo\" → base botafogo bare → ambiguous {rj, sp, pb} → default rj → botafogo-rj ✓ (FIFA's Botafogo IS Botafogo-RJ ✓).\n   FIFA \"Grêmio\" → base gremio ✓. \"Internacional\" → internacional-rs ✓. \"Vitória\" → base vitoria, states {ba} unambiguous → \"vitoria\" ✓ merges with match Vitória-BA ✓.\n   FIFA \"Bahia\" ✓. \"Chapecoense\" ✓. \"Cruzeiro\" ✓. \"Fluminense\" ✓. \"Santos\" → santos-sp ✓. \"Atlético Mineiro\" → alias atletico-mg ✓. \"Atlético Paranaense\" → atletico-pr ✓. \"Sport Club do Recife\" → sport ✓. \n\n   One conflict: FIFA \"Santos Laguna\" (Mexican) → base \"santos laguna\" distinct ✓. \"Club América\"? → if \"(MX)\" not present: base \"club america\"?? trailing filler: \"club\"→\"club america\"→ hmm tokens [\"club\",\"america\"] trailing \"america\" not filler → base \"club america\" — distinct from \"america\" ✗ MISSES alias. Add alias \"club america\" → (\"america\",\"mx\")? It's Club América (Mexico) — different from América Mineiro. Registering it creates base \"america\" with foreign:mx — fine, distinct id \"america-mx\". Add aliases \"club america\" → (\"america\", \"mx\") and \"club america mexico\" too. Wait but does fifa_data have \"Club América\"? FIFA 19: \"Club América\" exists. Let me add the alias defensively.\n\nDate parsing: formats \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\". Also \"NA\" → None. BRFB date \"2023-09-24\" ✓. What about BRFB dates with slashes? check: \"date\" column format? saw \"2023-09-24\". Hist \"29/03/2003\" ✓.\n\nGoal parsing: Libertadores goals are strings possibly \"NA\"/\"\" → to_int handles. BRFB goals floats \"1.0\" → int(float()) ✓. Half-time: \"ht_result\" is like \"DRAW\"?? saw ht_result: \"DRAW\", at_result \"DRAW\" — hmm those are DRAW strings, and \"ht_diff\"/\"at_diff\" numeric. Wait columns: ht_diff, at_diff, ht_result, at_result. Sample row: `ht_diff=0.0, at_diff=0.0, ht_result=DRAW, at_result=DRAW`. So ht_result/at_result are WIN/DRAW/LOSS labels, ht_diff/at_diff are goal differences at half time? \"ht_diff\" 0.0 with home_goal 1 away_goal 1 → HT was 0-0? So ht_diff = home goals at HT minus away? Not exactly retrievable both. I'll map: ht_result/at_result ∈ {WIN,DRAW,LOSS} → halftime home goals/away can't be fully derived from diff (diff only). Actually ht_diff + (ht home goals)? Can't get both from diff alone. I'll store half_time_diff = ht_diff and skip exact HT scores. Simpler: keep ht_diff/at_diff as-is? The spec table says \"ht_result, at_result — Half-time results\". I'll store half_time_home/away as None but keep a half_time_result string. Hmm — models.Match has half_time_home/away. Let me repurpose: store diff in attrs? Keep it simple: half_time_home = None; add field half_time_diff? I already froze the dataclass with half_time_home/away. Change to `half_time_diff: Optional[int]`. Let me update models.py accordingly... Actually simplest: parse ht_result (\"WIN\"/\"DRAW\"/\"LOSS\") into half_time_home/away? No. I'll change the two fields to `half_time_diff` + `half_time_label`. Let me edit models.py.\n\nFIFA height/weight parsing: height \"5'9\" → 5*30.48+9*2.54 = 175.26 → 175 cm. weight \"150lbs\" → 150*0.4536 = 68 kg. Values like \"159lbs\". Some may be missing.\n\nSource priority for dedup: Brasileirao_Matches(1), Libertadores_Matches(2), Brazilian_Cup_Matches(3), novo(4), BRFB(5)? Hmm — for Copa do Brasil overlap, Brazilian_Cup priority over BRFB ✓. For Série A, Brasileirao_Matches over novo over BRFB ✓.\n\nDeduplication detail: within same competition and same source there shouldn't be dupes. Across sources ✓.\n\nOne more consideration: dedup key with season — Copa do Brasil 2021 in Brazilian_Cup and BRFB 2021 ✓ same key comp/season/date/teams.\n\nAlso the NA Libertadores row (date NA, goals NA, teams Flamengo/Athletico): skip rows where teams missing OR (date is None AND goals are None). Actually keep rows with date but no goals? All rows in these files have goals mostly. Rule: skip if home/away empty, or both goals AND date missing. The NA row: date None + goals None → skipped ✓. But it had \"season=2020, stage=final\" hmm it was a real final leg 2 (Flamengo vs Athletico? no wait — the 2020 final was Palmeiras x Santos... The NA row says Flamengo vs Athletico — that was the 2020 SEMIFINAL? Actually Copa Libertadores 2020: semis were Palmeiras x Argentinos? no... Semifinals: Palmeiras vs ... and Santos vs ...; hmm Flamengo vs Athletico-PR was the 2020 QUARTER? no — actually Flamengo played Athletico in... I recall Flamengo eliminated Athletico-PR in the 2020 quarters? no wait — Athletico eliminated... doesn't matter!). The row lacks date+score → skip. But \"Find all Copa Libertadores finals\" — the 2020 final = Palmeiras 1-0 Santos (Jan 30, 2021 single match? the row shows 2020-01-30?? \"2020 2020-01-30 17:00:00 Palmeiras 1-0 Santos\" — date recorded as 2020-01-30 (should be 2021-01-30 but data says 2020). Fine — data as-is.)\n\nWait actually there's something interesting: that date \"2020-01-30\" — if it's wrong in data (real match 2021-01-30), whatever — we report data faithfully.\n\nCompetition tagging per file:\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv → BRASILEIRAO_A\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv → BRASILEIRAO_A\n- BR-Football-Dataset.csv: tournament col: \"Serie A\"→A, \"Serie B\"→B, \"Serie C\"→C, \"Copa do Brasil\"→COPA_DO_BRASIL\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv → COPA_DO_BRASIL\n- Libertadores_Matches.csv → LIBERTADORES\n\nAlso record round: Brasileirao \"round\" (int), cup \"round\" (str like \"1\"..\"8\" — or names? saw \"1\",\"2\"...), hist \"Rodada\", Libertadores stage. BRFB: no round.\n\nHist \"Vencedor\" — ignore (recomputable).\n\nNow service.py design (the meat). Methods (all return str for MCP; some expose structured internals for tests via separate functions or returning data + format separately). For tests I'd rather have service return STRUCTURED data and server format... but MCP tools need strings. Compromise: service methods return (title, lines) or formatted strings with a parallel `_structured` variant where tests need numbers. Actually for BDD tests, formatted strings can be asserted with `in` checks; but precise numeric assertions (points=90) need structure. I'll make service methods return dataclass results (e.g. TeamRecord, standings lists, match lists) AND provide `format_*` helpers, or methods return formatted string + tests use lower-level query methods. Plan:\n\nService query methods (structured):\n- `find_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=50)` → list[Match] (deduped, sorted by date desc) + total count. Return (matches, total).\n- `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` → dict with matches list + records per team.\n- `team_record(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=\"all\")` → TeamRecord (+ per-competition breakdown optionally).\n- `standings(competition, season)` → list[TeamRecord] sorted by pts, gf, gd.\n- `competition_summary(competition=None, season=None)` → stats dict (matches, avg goals, home/draw/away win rates, biggest win).\n- `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` → list[Match].\n- `best_records(venue=\"home\", competition=None, season=None, min_matches=5)` → list[TeamRecord] with venue info.\n- `derby_matches(season=None, competition=None)` → list[(derby_name, Match)].\n- `champion(competition, season)` → TeamRecord/champion info (league: top of standings; cup: winner of final).\n- `relegated(competition, season, n=4)` → bottom n of standings.\n- `seasons_for(competition)` → list[int].\n- `team_overview(team)` → dict: variants, competitions, seasons, record, finals? \n- Player: `find_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, min_age=None, max_age=None, limit=20, order=\"overall\")` → list[Player].\n- `player_profile(name)` → Player + formatted attrs.\n- `club_squad(club)` → list[Player] (via registry id match against FIFA clubs).\n- `top_brazilian_players(limit)` → list[Player].\n- `brazilians_at_brazilian_clubs()` → list[(club, count, avg)].\n- `fifa_club_for_team(team_id)` mapping.\n- `compare_seasons(competition, a, b)`.\n\nFormatting helpers produce the strings per spec examples.\n\nMCP tools map to these with formatting. ~18 tools.\n\nTime to write loaders.py. First update models.py half-time fields.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b42abd4001nSaL3sBx9G85an","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","messageID":"msg_05b40d23d001CE57OSnoyr3kYr"}}
