ved date-key dedup; of those, 76 are date mismatches of existing fixtures, leaving 36 fixtures not matching by (home,away,score)?? Maybe score discrepancies or BRFB has different team spellings resolving to different ids? Or BRFB 2019 Série A is incomplete (only some matches) — 112 matches where date differed... Let me think: BRFB Serie A 2019 has maybe ~340 matches. Hmm, whatever — the fixture-level dedup with score may still leave legit discrepancies (one source wrong score?). \n\nRather than guess, implement dedup v2 = fixture identity (comp, season, home, away, hg, ag) across sources (first/authoritative source wins). Then count 2019 → expect 380 + small residue. Residue = BRFB fixtures whose (home,away,score) doesn't match Brasileirao_Matches — maybe postponed matches that Brasileirao_Matches recorded with different... let me just run and inspect residue. If residue is small (~30), standings would still be corrupted for those teams (extra matches). \n\nAlternative stronger approach for standings: source preference per (competition, season) — pick ONE source entirely for league standings. E.g. 2019 Série A → Brasileirao_Matches only (380) ✓ perfect table. That's what I planned earlier (`league_matches`). Combined approach:\n1. dedup v2 across sources (fixture identity) for general listings — good enough, removes the bulk.\n2. `standings`/`champion`/`relegated` use single-source matches: for each (competition, season) choose the best source = source with most matches (ties → priority order). 2019 Série A: Brasileirao_Matches 380 vs BRFB ~340? — but wait after dedup v2 the stored data has 380 + residue; for single-source selection I must look at RAW per-source counts (before dedup). I'll track per (competition, season, source) counts on SoccerData at load: `matches_by_cs: dict[(competition, season), dict[source, list[Match]]]`? Simpler: store all matches with source, and helper `primary_source(competition, season)` = argmax over sources of raw count for that (comp, season), tie-break by MATCH_FILES priority order. Standings then filter `m.source == primary_source(...)`. For 2019: Brasileirao_Matches 380 vs BRFB (112 post-dedup, but raw ~340?) — raw counts: need raw per-source lists. I'll build `SoccerData.raw_by_comp_season: dict[(comp, season), dict[source, count]]` computed pre-dedup.\n\nHmm wait, actually — let me reconsider. With dedup v2 fixture-identity, is single-source still needed? The residue (36 fixtures in 2019) — those might be BRFB data-entry variants (score typos) creating phantom \"extra\" fixtures. For a benchmark, standings correctness matters (test: Flamengo 90pts). Single-source guarantees it. And single-source also handles the 2020-2022 overlap + 2023 BRFB-only + 2003-2011 novo-only consistently. I'll do BOTH: dedup v2 for listings, single-source for standings/records/champion/relegated/best-records.\n\nWhich source wins per (comp, season)? max raw count with priority tie-break. Série A 2012-2022: Brasileirao_Matches 380 vs novo 380 (2012-2019) — tie → priority: Brasileirao_Matches ✓. vs BRFB 2014-2022 (~340) → Brasileirao_Matches ✓. 2003-2011: novo only ✓. 2023: BRFB ✓. Copa do Brasil 2012-2021: Brazilian_Cup 108-162 vs BRFB 105-139: 2015: Brazilian_Cup 158 vs BRFB 139 → Brazilian_Cup ✓; 2020: 120 vs 117 ✓; 2022: BRFB only ✓; 2023: BRFB ✓. Nice — max-count rule works, priority tie-break handles 380=380.\n\nWhere do I get raw counts? Track in load_all phase 4: before dedup, count (competition, season, source). Store on SoccerData.\n\nAlso for team_record queries: should they use single-source too? \"What is Corinthians' home record in 2022?\" — single-source Brasileirao_Matches ✓ consistent. I'll use the same `primary` filter for all aggregation/stats (avg goals, best records, biggest wins) — consistent, non-double-counted. For match LISTINGS (search_matches, h2h, derbies) use deduped all-matches (max coverage).\n\nHalf of me wonders — h2h with dedup v2: fine.\n\nNow update loaders.py: dedup v2 + raw counts. Then check the residue and 2019 standings again.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b46f395001zDaBIDnseRMwPR","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","messageID":"msg_05b46614e001IDBiK1t1onVGnK"}}
