etition_stats(competition=None, season=None) -> CompStats\n    def biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10) -> list[Match]\n    def best_records(venue=\"home\", competition=None, season=None, min_matches=5) -> list[TeamRecord]\n    def compare_seasons(competition, season_a, season_b) -> str\n\n    # --- players\n    def search_players(...) -> list[Player]\n    def player_profile(name) -> Player\n    def club_squad(club) -> SquadResult\n    def top_brazilian_players(limit=10) -> list[Player]\n    def brazilians_at_brazilian_clubs() -> list[tuple[club, count, avg]]\n```\n\nAnd formatting helpers (module-level functions or service methods) producing the spec's answer formats. To keep it manageable, service methods return dataclasses; `format_*` functions render; server.py calls service + format. Tests can assert on both levels.\n\nStandings tie-break: points, wins, GD, GF (Brazilian rule).\n\nchampion(): league → top of standings; cup (Copa do Brasil, Libertadores) → analyze finals:\n- Libertadores: matches with stage == \"final\" for that season; compute aggregate; winner = aggregate winner; if tie → \"decided on penalties (not recorded)\" with both finalists listed. Report.\n- Copa do Brasil: finals = matches in the highest round of that season (round numbers are strings \"1\"..\"8\"); two legs; aggregate; penalties caveat.\nAlso for champion of Série B/C — standings-based ✓.\n\nrelegated: bottom 4 of standings (Série A context: 4 relegated).\n\nteam_record: filtered matches from DEDUPED set? Or primary-source? For consistency with standings, use primary-source filtering when season+competition given? Hmm — \"Corinthians home record in 2022\" — 2022 Série A primary = Brasileirao_Matches ✓. But team_record for a team across ALL competitions/seasons (no filter) — deduped set is right (coverage). Rule: when competition AND season are both specified → primary-source matches; else → deduped all. Wait — what about competition specified, season not (e.g. \"Corinthians record in Copa do Brasil all-time\")? Different sources cover different seasons of the cup — deduped set is correct (each season's fixtures appear once). The double-count risk only exists per (comp, season) overlap. So: apply primary-source filter ONLY when both comp and season given. Hmm — but consider comp=Série A, season=None: 2012-2019 fixtures deduped by fixture identity ✓ no double count. ✓ OK: primary filter when comp+season; else deduped.\n\nWait — but dedup by fixture-identity isn't perfect: score typos between sources create phantom fixtures (the 36-ish residue in 2019). For team_record with comp+season → primary ✓ clean. For all-time records → small phantom residue, acceptable for demo.\n\nActually, hmm, for consistency: what about `competition_stats(competition=BRASILEIRAO_A, season=None)` — \"average goals per match in the Brasileirão\" — all-time across seasons, deduped set has residue... The residue is small (~36 phantoms out of ~7600) — negligible. But when season given → primary source ✓ exact.\n\n`avg goals Brasileirão`: spec example says 2.47. Our data 2003-2023... fine whatever it computes.\n\nbest_records(venue=\"home\"): \"Which team has the best home record?\" — all-time or season-filtered; use deduped set (all seasons) or comp+season primary. I'll offer season param optional.\n\nderbies(): deduped matches where pairing in DERBIES (unordered).\n\nteam_overview(team): display, id, name variants seen (registry internal? I didn't keep raw variants per canonical id in registry... registry._provisional has pid→display raw. For variants I can reconstruct: canonical id → set of raw names. Add method to registry: `variants(team_id)`. Let me add: registry keeps `_provisional` with remap available → variants(team_id) = {entry display for pid where remap[pid]==team_id}. Add to normalizer.)\n\nAlso competitions played + seasons + overall record + FIFA squad note (cross-file!). \n\nplayer_profile: full detail + attrs (top skills).\n\nsearch_players