 def primary_source(self, competition: str, season: Optional[int]) -> Optional[str]:\n-    \"\"\"The single most authoritative source for (competition, season).\"\"\"\n+    \"\"\"The single most authoritative source for (competition, season).\n+\n+    Priority-first: the first source (in MATCH_FILES authority order)\n+    that recorded this competition+season wins. A pure max-match-count\n+    rule would misfire where a lower-authority file over-reports (e.g.\n+    BR-Football files 491 rows under \"2021 Série A\" because it labels\n+    season-2020 tail matches played in Jan/Feb 2021 by calendar year).\n+    \"\"\"\n     counts = self.data.source_counts_by_season.get((competition, season))\n     if not counts:\n         return None\n-    return max(counts.items(), key=lambda kv: (kv[1], -MATCH_FILES.index(kv[0])))[0]\n+    for source in MATCH_FILES:\n+        if source in counts:\n+            return source\n+    return next(iter(counts))\n \n def primary_matches(self, competition: str, season: Optional[int]) -> list[Match]:\n     \"\"\"Matches for one competition+season from one authoritative source.\"\"\"\n     source = self.primary_source(competition, season)\n","additions":12,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer_mcp/service.py","time":{"start":1788238399713,"end":1788238399716}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The fallback heuristic works perfectly with the data:\n- 2021: BRFB last 2 = Athletico-PR 1-2 Atlético-MG (Dec 16) and Atlético-MG 4-0 Athletico-PR (Dec 12) — real 2021 final (Atlético-MG champion, agg 6-1) ✓. Note Brazilian_Cup 2021 stops Aug 5 with NA scores (unplayed scheduled fixtures).\n- 2022: Corinthians 0-0 Flamengo / Flamengo 1-1 Corinthians — 2022 final (Flamengo champion on... agg 1-1?? Real 2022 final: Flamengo beat Corinthians on penalties after 1-1 agg? Hmm — actually 2022 Copa do Brasil final: Corinthians 0-0 Flamengo, Flamengo 1-1 Corinthians → agg 1-1, Flamengo won on penalties?? No wait — 2022 Copa do Brasil champion was Flamengo. Agg 1-1 → penalties (not in data) → my champion() will report \"aggregate tied 1-1... decided on penalties, not recorded\". Accurate to the data! Good — honest answer.\n- 2023: Flamengo 0-1 São Paulo / São Paulo 1-1 Flamengo → agg SP 2-1 → São Paulo champion ✓ (real 2023: São Paulo won Copa do Brasil ✓).\n\nAlso note: for 2021 cup, Brazilian_Cup's round-4 rows have NA scores (unplayed). My last-N-by-date fallback picks PLAYED matches only (I'll filter played). ✓\n\nNow — the priority-first primary source change also affects: 2021 cup primary = Brazilian_Cup (108 matches, mostly played through round 4... wait rounds 1-4 = 40+20+32+16=108, with the round-4 ones unplayed/NA. So primary (Brazilian_Cup) is truncated, but that only matters for standings-like aggregates (cups don't have standings) — fine.\n\nBut 2021 Série A: priority-first → Brasileirao_Matches (380 rows, played?). Let me check played count for 2021 Série A in Brasileirao_Matches: the dataset was probably captured late 2021 — might have ~380 complete or truncated. Earlier unplayed counter (buggy primary) showed 2021: 212 — that was BRFB-primary leftovers. Let me recheck after fix.\n\nAlso 2016: 1 unplayed in Brasileirao_Matches? Earlier: unplayed per season {2016: 1, 2022: 81} — 2016 has 1 unplayed (a postponed match never recorded as played, e.g. Chapecoense-related cancelation after the plane crash — the match Vitória x Chapecoense? hmm — actually the 2016 last round: Inter x Chapecoense? — Laio; whatever). With priority-first, 2016 primary = Brasileirao_Matches ✓ same as before.\n\nChanges to make in service.py:\n1. `primary_source`: priority-first (first source in MATCH_FILES order that has any matches for (comp, season)); if none → None. Drop the max-count rule.\n\nHmm wait — is priority-first ALWAYS right? Consider Série A 2023: Brasileirao_Matches has NO 2023 ✓ → falls through Libertadores (comp mismatch, 0) → Cup (0) → novo (0) → BRFB (377) ✓. Copa do Brasil 2014-2021: Brazilian_Cup has matches ✓ primary (BRFB also has — but Brazilian_Cup is the dedicated cup file) ✓. Série A 2003-2011: novo ✓. Série B/C: only BRFB ✓. Libertadores: Libertadores_Matches ✓ always. Good — priority-first is right everywhere here.\n\n2. `finals()` rework for Copa do Brasil:\n   - pool = deduped matches for comp+season.\n   - round-data candidates: matches with parseable round; if the max-round group has ≤ 4 matches → those are the finals.\n   - else → played matches sorted by date; take last 2; if same team pair → both; else last 1. (Guard: if <2 matches, take what's there.)\n   Also apply to leagues? The current league branch (final round of season) is mostly meaningless; keep simple: leagues have no \"finals\" — return [] and champion() uses standings for leagues anyway. Actually — for leagues, the \"final round\" concept: last ROUND of the league season = every team plays; not \"finals\". I'll return [] for leagues... but the tool docstring says \"Find final matches of a competition\" — for leagues explain none. Let me keep league branch returning [] with the champion tool relying on standings. Hmm — but `finals(\"Brasileirão\")` returning empty might confuse; the guard message will say \"League competitions don't have finals; use standings/champion\". I'll implement that as the empty-string case with a helpful message in the tool.\n\n   For Libertadores ✓ stage-based as-is.\n\n3. `champion()` cup branch — fix the multi-line formatting bug (one line per final match). Also handle single-match finals (aggregate from 1 match ✓ already works), and >2 final matches (2013 Libertadores had 2 finals ✓; what if finals() returns 3+? e.g. 2021 cup fallback could pick 2 ✓). Keep robust for any count: aggregate per team across all finals; pick top two by goals.\n\n   Current implementation unpacks exactly 2 teams — if finals() somehow returns matches with 3+ teams (weird data), it crashes. Make it robust: sort teams by aggregate goals, compare top two. Edge: fallback picks last 2 matches with same pairing → 2 teams ✓; single final → 2 teams ✓; stage-based finals 2 teams ✓.\n\n4. Also fix `champion` league line: `f\"{season or ''} {comp} champion...\"` with `.replace(\"  \", \" \")` hack — fine.\n\nAdditionally, `_aggregation_matches(comp=None, season=None)` → returns full deduped list (all comps) — for competition_stats with no args (all competitions) ✓ intended.\n\nOne more thing — `search_matches` when competition given but season NOT: `pool = self._aggregation_matches(comp, None)` → deduped set filtered by comp ✓.\n\nNow about 2021 Série A played count under priority-first — check after fix. Also there's still the \"unplayed matches with ?-?\" showing in match listings — acceptable (they're scheduled fixtures). In h2h listing they show too ✓ fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider `finals` for Copa do Brasil when BOTH round-data max-round ≤4 matches AND later BRFB dates exist (2021: Brazilian_Cup max round 4 = 16 matches → not ≤4 → fallback ✓ correct!). And 2012: max round 6 = 2 matches ✓ finals. \n\nEdge: what if max-round group has ≤4 matches but it's NOT the final due to truncation? E.g. hypothetical season truncated at round 7 (semifinals = 4 matches) → misidentified as finals. In our data: 2012-2020 max rounds all have exactly 2 matches and ARE finals ✓. 2021 max round 4 has 16 → fallback ✓. OK.\n\nImplement now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b5088d6001yU6uo4CvymLl3o","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","messageID":"msg_05b4fdc0a001Y3jyXuc3fR60WL"}}
