ration order: the FIRST alias containing or contained... aliases dict order: \"brasileirao\", \"brasileirão\", \"serie a\", \"série a\", \"serie a (brasileirao)\", \"campeonato brasileiro\", \"serie b\" ← comes before \"série b\". Check \"brasileirão série b\" vs alias \"brasileirao\": \"brasileirao\" in \"brasileirão série b\"? NO (accent difference). \"brasileirão\" in \"brasileirão série b\"? YES → returns BRASILEIRAO_A!!! \n\nSo `standings(\"Brasileirão Série B\", 2023)` → resolve_competition mangles it to Série A! That's the bug: resolve_competition isn't idempotent for canonical names containing \"brasileirão\".\n\nFix: check exact canonical names FIRST:\n\n```python\ncanonicals = set(COMPETITION_ALIASES.values())\nif name.strip() in canonicals: return name.strip()\n```\n\nAlso the fuzzy loop is sloppy — \"serie a\" in \"serie a (brasileirao)\"?? whatever. Fix: 1) exact canonical; 2) exact alias; 3) fuzzy containment (alias in key only, preferring longer aliases); 4) canonical containment.\n\nLet me rewrite resolve_competition robustly:\n\n```python\ndef resolve_competition(self, name):\n    if not name: return None\n    raw = name.strip()\n    key = raw.lower()\n    canonicals = set(COMPETITION_ALIASES.values())\n    if raw in canonicals: return raw          # idempotent for canonical names\n    if key in COMPETITION_ALIASES: return COMPETITION_ALIASES[key]\n    # unaccented canonical match\n    from .normalizer import strip_accents\n    sa = strip_accents(key)\n    for canonical in canonicals:\n        if strip_accents(canonical.lower()) == sa: return canonical\n    # fuzzy: longest alias contained in key\n    best = None\n    for alias, canonical in COMPETITION_ALIASES.items():\n        if alias in key or key in alias:\n            if best is None or len(alias) > len(best[0]): best = (alias, canonical)\n    if best: return best[1]\n    # canonical containment: \"libertadores\" in \"copa libertadores\"\n    for canonical in sorted(canonicals, key=len, reverse=True):\n        if key in strip_accents(canonical.lower()) or strip_accents(canonical.lower()) in key... \n```\n\nHmm — careful with \"brasileirão série b\" key vs canonical \"Brasileirão Série B\": strip accents both → \"brasileirao serie b\" == \"brasileirao serie b\" ✓ exact canonical (unaccented) match ✓ returns B.\n\n\"copa do brasil\" key: exact alias ✓. \"libertadores\" exact alias ✓. \"brasileirao\" alias ✓. \"Serie A\" → \"serie a\" ✓. \"campeonato\" → fuzzy: alias \"campeonato brasileiro\" in key? no; key in alias? \"campeonato\" in \"campeonato brasileiro\" ✓ → A. OK.\n\nLet me implement this cleanly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b5354220014rT6oE15TmTZX8","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","messageID":"msg_05b52dc83001qbFz58vmfnpPHf"}}
