{"type":"text","timestamp":1788239881587,"sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","part":{"id":"prt_05b6705d1001SF7zCN0jQuE2gf","messageID":"msg_05b66f95f001ocEDuZpl1cHWgZ","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccfc0bffexSFu2vIRQjobrF","type":"text","text":"Done. **198 tests pass in ~6s, lint clean, MCP server verified over both in-memory and stdio transports.**\n\n## What was built\n\nA Brazilian Soccer MCP server per `TASK.md`, structured as:\n\n- **`brazilian_soccer_mcp/`** — models, team-name normalizer, CSV loaders, query service, formatting, MCP tool surface\n- **`server.py`** — stdio entrypoint (20 MCP tools, `mcp` SDK v2.x)\n- **`tests/`** — 198 BDD given/when/then pytest scenarios, including end-to-end MCP client sessions\n\n## Key engineering problems solved\n\n1. **Club identity vs. name variants** — the datasets contain genuinely different clubs sharing base names (Atlético-MG/PR/GO, Flamengo-RJ vs Flamengo-PI, Botafogo-RJ/SP). The normalizer keeps the state suffix exactly when a base is ambiguous, unifies spellings otherwise (\"Palmeiras-SP\" ≡ \"Palmeiras\", \"Atlético Mineiro\" ≡ \"Atlético-MG\"), and never merges foreign clubs (\"Barcelona-EQU\" ≠ \"FC Barcelona\"). Also fixed a subtle case-sensitivity bug that made bare-name resolution non-deterministic across runs.\n2. **Cross-source deduplication** — Série A 2012-2019 is in three files with conflicting dates; dedup keys on fixture identity (pairing+score), and per-season aggregations use a single authoritative source so standings never double-count.\n3. **Cup finals recovery** — Libertadores finals via stage data; Copa do Brasil via highest round, with a last-played-dates fallback for truncated seasons (2021-2023), correctly recovering champions like Atlético-MG 2021 (6-1 agg) and honestly reporting the 2022 penalty-shootout tie.\n\nVerified against real-world ground truth: Flamengo 2019 (90 pts), São Paulo 2023 cup, Vitória 2023 Série B, 2020 relegations — all match.","time":{"start":1788239873489,"end":1788239881583}}}
